/** worker-slot-12 — siman 467 editorial batch 12 fixes (45 blocks) */
export const FIXES = {
  "peri-megadim/part-001.txt": {
    "4:ב": "(ב) And indeed from responsum of the Ran siman 59 it is difficult — except there too it implies the reason like Rashba that it is common in sheaves that rain drips on upper parts of the stack and in the middle water does not reach — unlike in the Agur incident. And what the Mechaber wrote in seif 3 if their appearance did not change — behold if changed forbidden — Avi HaEzri and Levush also forbidden to retain them — behold they did not come in a ship; even if appearance changed if there are no others one might say permitted:",
    "5:א": "(א) There is Taz: behold flour in honey without moistening is like siman 462:6 except only for eating thus; but to make a drink from the start — even flour in honey all that initially lacks sixty then leavens in water before cooking well; however before Pesach permitted and nullified in sixty with water; and from the fifth hour until nightfall if known flour was mixed or small vessels customary to mix flour and concern perhaps no sixty against flour or perhaps in one place not blended well — behold for us flavor is not flavor in other prohibitions; and soaked in soft honey is like soaked — see Yoreh De'ah 105 and 104; if so honey became nevelah and afterward needs sixty against all the honey and there are not sixty in water against all the honey for it is Yom Tov and enhanced. Therefore for one who permits small vessels to make a drink from them before the fifth hour — from the fifth until nightfall not from what we said; however from not nullifying prohibition permitted all where there is safek whether they mix — similar to vinegar — see Yoreh De'ah 114 Shach 21 and 116:28 — see there; except from nevelah such — perhaps fine flour or flour mixed with water as he wrote; and b'dieved if cooked thus one might say in pressing circumstances if not known — perhaps we do not say flavor is not flavor l'chatchila must cook before fifth hour; and large hive barrels not customary to forge — even rooms not recognizable — Taz's view implies permission even to eat during Pesach that honey; and so to cook during Pesach from those large barrels not customary to forge — as appears from Rambam's conclusion that not to eat only from the hive in a barrel — implies permitted to eat on Pesach from it and so drink if fit for drinking on Yom Tov else no melacha of another's Yom Tov. And behold what he proved permitted to make drink on Pesach from large barrels — though obvious since even for eating Rambam permits — all the more so drink — one might say drink is stricter lest flour inside and not blended well and Yom Tov in water in any amount on Pesach unlike eating in their substance fruit juice does not leaven — therefore he wrote no concern at all in large barrels where they never mix flour:",
    "5:ב": "(ב) And what he wrote regarding saffron — at first since we see saffron dissolved in the cooked dish and nothing remains — necessarily even if nevelah meat threads are mixed custom is to invert and all becomes like saffron — therefore we follow present state; somewhat strained to explain Taz's words; also there is concern Yom Tov — see there; and Magen Avraham letter 3 and Chayyei Adam here letter 16 wrote novel fat that did not change to a permitted thing — nevertheless one might say we do not permit forbidden fat for it is not such a change as blood which the Torah forbade and did not change to another appearance — and blood that was cooked is permitted for most codifiers and so is the halachah unlike forbidden fat; no verse needed — see there. And if known flour or fine flour was put in honey and fell into a dish in less than sixty — Chayyei Adam relying on leniency of Rabbenu Yonah that it turned to honey did not lose — see there and Magen Avraham letter 11 — meaning when it stood some time in the honey — for in short time it does not turn to honey:",
    "6:א": "(א) And know in Yoreh De'ah 84 and Shach letter 37 honey is accustomed to preserve and sustain things hidden in it; as Rashba wrote in responsum 80: whole things preserve; like Bava Batra regarding Herod who hid seven years in honey; and cut things quickly consumed — see there; therefore whole grains found in honey on Pesach even though long time — soaked preserves; and soaked in soft honey honey becomes chametz and forbids the dish in any amount — no distinction whether there was living soul in it or one grain unlike fine flour long time turned to honey — permitted b'dieved as written. And it will be explained in Magen Avraham 7:",
    "6:ב": "(ב) And figs — Taz wrote flour — one might say fruit juice and fine flour concern — see Levush; and in my humble opinion zafarin too forbidden lest substitution; and see Levush: honey from a barrel is common and we are not concerned for substitution unlike zafarin in my view not common — they say it came from overseas; and so dried fruits from Israel people buy known and common to dry for Pesach and no substitution. And fruit a gentile dried with chametz bread in one oven — from aroma one might say b'dieved forbid the mixture — we hold bread aroma does not forbid — see siman 447; nevertheless one might say touched chametz bread. And raisins red — not eaten; small black ones some permit — see there and Magen Avraham letter 8 and it will be explained there:",
    "6:ג": "(ג) And know in honey and wine if it stood long — one might say split from wine and honey and permitted unlike frying in fat which sense testifies splitting does not occur from fat frying — if found split in the rounds one might say common and attribute from elsewhere; but in softened alone we permit for us — as stated; and it will be explained letter 12; and in Mishnah Berurah regarding roasted chicken with grain found — also:",
    "7:_": "And figs — Taz wrote flour; fruit juice and fine flour concern; Levush; zafarin forbidden lest substitution; dried fruit from Israel not substituted:",
    "8:א": "(א) And tsuker kandil — Taz wrote in sour vats; stam vessels Avi HaEzri and Yom Tov against Yom Tov is from siman 447 from this; and end of siman; and what tsuker Candia they eat on last Yom Tov — hot tsuker without real cooking; experts cook in special vessels — product is cane grown in ground boiled in water extracted like flour — so merchants said; Magen Avraham letter 9; l'chatchila practice to sell even hot tsuker; b'dieved not forbidden; even our kandil cooked in sour vats if stood — not forbidden after Pesach for nevelah ben nevelah; Avi HaEzri siman 447 there. And Taz: not common among us — properly concerned for substitution like figs from his garden; and in my view zafarin letter 9 and 7 — recognizable distinct form:",
    "8:ב": "(ב) And if pressing circumstances on Pesach market day and much selling tsuker — one might say permitted to sell hot tsuker on pressing circumstances; merchants testified no flour mixed in hot tsuker — everything by custom; where practice prohibition do not permit before them; where practice permission permitted. Now Israelites make hot tsuker for Pesach with some change lest substitution — well done and fixed per great ones of the generation and Peri Chadash. See Levush: some lenient in kandil tsuker; on last Yom Tov do not be so stringent — see there:",
    "9:_": "Saffron — Taz and Levush; see letter 7 distinction between common here and not common — concerned:"
  },
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "Siman 467 seif 8 in the gloss — not to eat any dried fruits; the law of orange peel is like dried fruits — Chayyei Adam general rule 127 seif 2:",
    "2:_": "Magen Avraham seif 8 here — it is an uncertainty because many, etc.; one should examine — but in siman 447 too it is an uncertainty, perhaps the pan is a second vessel, and nevertheless they forbid taste imparted by taste:",
    "3:_": "Magen Avraham seif 12 — rabbinic prohibition in any amount, we follow uncertainty to leniency; in my humble opinion from the words of Tosafos Chullin 97 in the name of Rava it is clear that he holds that in uncertainty one forbids in any amount:",
    "4:_": "There — and perhaps they split open; practical difference for us when they did not soften:",
    "5:_": "Magen Avraham seif 15 — that it is baked — a small thing does not leaven; only my inclination is not toward that, that a small thing does not leaven the entire dough — so it should read:",
    "6:_": "Seif 12 — and there is one who forbids if — see in Chiddushei Rabbi Akiva Eiger Yoreh De'ah siman 91 in the Biurim seif 3:",
    "7:_": "Magen Avraham seif 19 — he forbade him because there was a crack; in my humble opinion, nevertheless it is proven that the water is forbidden, for otherwise what of it that there is a crack — certainly nothing of the wheat actually entered the crack; if so the water is forbidden because it releases even in cold; only the meat is perhaps permitted because it does not release and absorb, and it is forbidden only because of the crack that the water enters the crack; and so too as Magen Avraham wrote siman 447 seif 9; and if so automatically if they cooked in forbidden water:",
    "8:_": "Magen Avraham seif 22 — afterward it broke — forbidden; and this is per the opinion of the Taz Yoreh De'ah end of siman 83 that if he chewed it is digestion, but if he did not chew, even if afterward it was crushed and diminished, it is not digestion; but the opinion of the Shach there is the reverse — that even according to one who holds one does not derive prohibition from impurity, and if he chewed it is not digestion, nevertheless in what diminished from what it absorbed there is digestion — see there:",
    "9:_": "Magen Avraham seif 25 — and so too the Derech HaMelech; in my humble opinion, nevertheless, although if we come to permit the chicken from the law of sixty rabbinic — perhaps it was in the matzah — with this the reasoning would be correct, since this permission applies also to the matzah that perhaps it was in the chicken — automatically both are forbidden like the case in siman 111; and if so the permission of sixty rabbinic is removed because I say differently — it is forty against sixty rabbinic; but nevertheless the permission of double uncertainty is not removed, for regarding the chicken there is no double uncertainty — for if there were uncertainty here in the matzah there is no double uncertainty; and if so, since if this were uncertainty that forbids, we could permit the chicken from double uncertainty — for the matzah is certainly forbidden from Torah-level sixty — if so from the Torah because it is rabbinic, and it would also be on the matzah from rabbinic sixty; and this is indeed the permission removed from rabbinic sixty because of \"why do you see\" — but the permission of double uncertainty was not removed — end quote; and it is different from the case in siman 139 where on the entire house there is double uncertainty, and again \"why do you see\" applies — end quote:"
  },
  "shaarei-teshuvah/part-001.txt": {
    "11:_": "We establish presumptive prohibition. And see in Be'er HaGolah general rule 4 siman 10 that in Egypt they bring wheat in ships and the sailors mix water, mud, and dust into them; even though some wheat leavens they are accustomed to leniency because they are nullified in the rest of the wheat; and similarly when they stack the stack after they thresh it with a threshing sledge, dew falls on the wheat night after night for at least about a month until they winnow and bring to storage — and dew leavens; but they are accustomed to leniency because it returns and revives, and they bake even on Pesach and are not concerned for what Mahari`t wrote in part Aleph Chayyim siman 1 that one must bake before Pesach; or one can resolve Mahari`t that in dry such as flour one may not be lenient — see there; and therefore one should not be stringent for them when some matter occurs that depends on this dispute of returning and reviving, such as the dispute of shaking in a sieve that I brought above siman 461 — see there:",
    "12:_": "Regarding ordinary wheat. Shaarei Teshuvah — and he wrote in responsum of Maharan Rotenburg siman 35 that the Gaon Maharam son of the Mechaber answered regarding rains that descended on heaps — there are wheat kernels that sprouted, but if there are sixty against the permitted ones, one may permit even l'chatchila not in a time of distress to grind them, and there is no matter of nullifying prohibition without intent, as written in Yoreh De'ah siman 84; only the Bach wrote that one must bake before Pesach; and this matter of siman 467 regarding grain placed in an attic — since it was not ground and baked before Pesach it is dry with dry and returns and revives on Pesach — therefore one should sell it before Pesach like other chametz mixtures — see there; and see in responsum Me'il Tzedakah siman 69 that he wrote in the year 5472 there were rains during harvest days and we saw that wheat harvested a day or two later for matzah of the commandment were finished and fully dried, and we were uncertain perhaps they were thus finished and dried at the time rain was on the ground; the Rashba ruled that it accepts leavening even while attached to the ground, and he brought that one need not be concerned for this even if they dried completely in their husk, unless when rains were abundant day and night — strong rain that knocks the stalks and sheaves to the ground and rain drips on them strongly, and it is as one who rests barrels full of water — but with rain that is not so strong and does not last a long time one should not be stringent unless we see them split; and know that they required guarding only from harvest time and not from when they finished while attached; and further, behold dew does not stop and falls on them every day, and it is not mentioned in any posek, etc. — see there:",
    "14:_": "Dried fruits. Shaarei Teshuvah and in Noda B'Yehuda Mahadura Tinyana siman 91 he wrote on what Magen Avraham wrote to permit even in an oven in which bread was baked, etc. — perhaps Magen Avraham did not pay attention to see that it is customary to scatter much flour under the bread, and perhaps some remained in the oven in its own form, etc. (in my humble opinion one can say that most of that flour remained stuck to the bread that was only at the time of its going from the peel to the oven, and if a little falls from there the heat of the oven makes it ash and in any case very charred until no taste of chametz remains when they put the fruits in the oven — uncertain); and in the land of Poland in all the provinces they are not accustomed to permit, neither in prunes called plumen nor in apples nor in dried pears — only what was dried in one's house and in one's oven for the festival of Pesach; and when he came to this country they were accustomed regarding fruits bought from those ovens standing in the gardens of the nobles where they do not bake there, but from those brought to sell in the market there is no permission; and in lands of the Ger they were accustomed to prohibition even from gardens — it is difficult to permit even though there is permission on that which is in the garden to make wine from them before Pesach; nevertheless one may not permit if there is concern that a breach will emerge to treat customs and prohibitions lightly — see there:",
    "15:_": "And tsuker. Shaarei Teshuvah — and see in responsum Noda B'Yehuda part 1 siman 23 and in Mahadura Tinyana siman 72 that he wrote regarding powdered tsuker brought in barrels and some are concerned for mixtures of flour, and also perhaps the barrels are from barrels in which they carry flour to that country where it grows; and he wrote that if the seller is among the great merchants one should not protest in a place where the custom is to be lenient, and in any case they should be careful to take that powder immediately when the gentile opens the barrels and take from the middle of the barrels — see there; and see there in Chayyei Adam siman 24 that he wrote that in the place of its growth one need not be concerned for forgery because there flour is twice as expensive as tsuker, therefore the custom when it is brought in a ship to Hamburg or Amsterdam is that the rabbi sends his agents to seal the barrels and from there they bring them sealed with certification from the rabbi — see there; and see in She'elat Ya'avetz part 2 siman 65, etc., that kistin tsuker from the note — its permission was released by our teacher the Noda B'Yehuda; and see in responsum Tiferet Tzvi simanim 18 and 19 that he also wrote to permit that brought in barrels from distant places from the place of tsuker growth itself, such as to merchants of the land of Israel, and the tsuker is bronze dye and one need not be concerned for a mixture of flour, which is a distant concern and is not equal in appearance; but if it is not bronze dye one should be somewhat stringent even though from its place of growth there flour is cheap — nevertheless there is a decree lest it be confused; one should also buy from other merchants who store on the rabbi's route and pour from vessel to vessel, and there is concern that perhaps where flour is cheap they mix flour there; we are concerned even for a gentile merchant — see there; and it appears that in a place of need or loss one may permit in this when it is known that all year barrels of tsuker come to him from their place of growth as they are even if not bronze, and they give him a certification on the barrels that come to him on this route, and there is no concern for confusion to buy from one who has no certification, for it is known that certainly no certification was given to him except after investigation that there is no concern — unlike one who has no certification, the buyer should be concerned for himself:",
    "16:_": "{In the gloss seif 10} They are accustomed to burn everything; and see siman 447 and in the Acharonim what is written on this; and see in responsum Chacham Tzvi siman 86 that his opinion is that even on the seventh of Pesach if a wheat kernel is found in a cooked dish or in a chicken they force them with vessels until the end of Yom Tov, and then they eat the cooked dish and the chicken and burn the wheat kernel — unlike what the Acharonim strained to be stringent to burn it through a gentile, for even on chol ha-mo'ed there is no prohibition here (Beit Yosef); and what they were accustomed to burn it is because of damage; and on the seventh of Pesach forcing with vessels suffices — see there; and see there that if the gentile himself comes to take it, then on the seventh of Pesach it is forbidden to protest against him taking it even regarding rabbinic prohibition in any amount; but on the eighth day of Pesach one can protest against him:",
    "6:_": "In a river. Shaarei Teshuvah — and he wrote in responsum of Mahari`t Tzahalon siman 45 that a crate full of wheat stood and he passed a bowl of water from place to place and the water fell on the crate, and it was before Pesach; since there was only one bowl that does not contain enough to soak even a tenth of the wheat and they flowed via an incline — therefore if there are cracks in the edges of the box between board and board through which water flows and does not collect so that the wheat becomes soaked, they were permitted; and all the more so here where it fell only on a little, one can say it was nullified before its time of prohibition; and if there are no cracks there, one discards what is at the edges of the box in the place where there is concern that water collected there, and the rest is permitted — see there; and it appears that l'chatchila one should grind the wheat before Pesach:",
    "9:_": "Forbidden. Shaarei Teshuvah — and see in the place of Shmuel siman 58 that he wrote in resolving the difficulty of the Taz on the Rama, he brought as the ruling of the Rama — see there:"
  },
  "turei-zahav/part-001.txt": {
    "1:א": "(א) Or water fell upon them. In the Tur he wrote: and they have concern for leavening and it is not recognizable in them — end of his words; for if it were recognizable there is no concern, for the buyer will see the leavening:",
    "1:ב": "(ב) And one informs him so that he will eat them, etc. In our Gemara it says except one — Rava: let us buy them measure by measure for Israelites so that we consume them before Pesach. And this seif is the language of the Rambam, and his version does not read to Israelites but to a gentile one sells little by little; but to an Israelite one may sell even a large amount once one informs him of the matter — so wrote the Maggid. But the Rosh and Tur have the reading to Israelites, and the author of the Ittur wrote: even if he did not inform the Israelite it is permitted as long as he can eat it before Pesach; and the Rambam wrote that one must inform him, and the Tur wrote, and it is reasonable like the author of the Ittur — from the fact that it is permitted only for a small amount, if one informs him even a large amount is also permitted, etc. Rashal wrote, and one might say: have our mouths been sealed so that we say to the Israelite that it is chametz — why should we rely on the permission that it will be consumed before Pesach not for the need of the matter, since we can inform him? Therefore it appears that it deals with informing, and nevertheless one may not sell more lest he forget and come to a stumbling block — end of his words. And I wonder greatly at the words of the rabbi, for the Rambam holds that one must inform him, and for him it is truly permitted even a large amount, as he states here explicitly; and the Tur's difficulty is according to his reading that reads to Israelites — therefore he questioned even a large amount; but according to what the Maggid said that the Rambam's version does not read to Israelites — the Tur's difficulty is resolved, for if so the large amount is also permitted to an Israelite, only that Rava teaches us that even for a gentile, where informing is not applicable, nevertheless one sells to him measure by measure; and it appears that one should be concerned for the Tur's reading, which is the reading of most books — namely to Israelites, and it is not permitted except with informing; and nevertheless one should not sell except a little, for the reason that when he sells a large amount and will rely on the informing there is concern lest he forget to inform one of the buyers and a stumbling block will come — unlike when he sells a little to a gentile, where there is a double uncertainty that perhaps he will not sell it to an Israelite; and if you say he will sell it to him, perhaps he will eat it before Pesach, as stated above — for practical halachah:",
    "10:_": "On the eighth day of Pesach, etc. It appears all the more so regarding taste imparted, for we are stringent on Pesach as written in siman 446 — it is permitted to keep it on the last Yom Tov, for it is lenient regarding rabbinic prohibition in any amount:",
    "11:_": "And so they practice during Pesach. The distinction of during Pesach is in the Beit Yosef, who questioned what the Tur wrote in the name of the Rosh regarding a wheat kernel found embedded in dough or matzah — even though permitted, etc., and we are not concerned lest it was in water, because Israelites are accustomed to guard the dough, etc. — it implies that if it had been in water the matzot would be forbidden; and afterward he wrote an incident where an embedded wheat kernel was found in a vessel of water and the water is permitted; and one might say that here it deals during Pesach and that incident deals on the eve of Pesach; and they already questioned on this, for certainly that incident also deals on Pesach as the Tur implies; and I wondered — for per the Tur, behold the Tur wrote afterward and so Avi HaEzri permitted wheat that fell into a pit, and wrote they do not leaven, etc.; and it appears the case is not comparable to the proof, for that one is specifically in dough, but an embedded wheat kernel certainly leavens in cold water like a stalk that leavens in its husk; but it appears to permit from the Tur because the water is cold the wheat kernel does not impart taste in them, and even full chametz that fell into a pit is permitted for this reason — end of his words; behold the wheat kernel is forbidden in cold and does not forbid — therefore there is difficulty on the Rosh, for if the wheat kernel had been in water it would certainly have been forbidden, and you have no permission in matzah except because the matzah is not forbidden in cold; with this it appears the Rosh holds this is specifically when resting in cold, but in kneading the dough warms somewhat through the work and compresses the wheat kernel in the hand or vessel — certainly there is concern it squeezed some taste from it and forbids; and all the more so in baking; and if so it is not necessary to distinguish between before Pesach and during Pesach. And in Maharil it is written: a wheat kernel was found on the eve of Pesach in matzah — Semak permitted removal of the place; and if a wheat kernel was found on Pesach in matzah before baking, Maharil ruled to burn it by itself in a bonfire; and if they removed the wheat kernel from it and baked it, they must burn it, and the rest of the matzot baked with it he permitted. And a wheat kernel found in dough — the entire dough is forbidden; so ruled Maharash, and Maharshal agreed in responsum siman 79, because through kneading it leavens and gives some taste; and so too the Rama's words here, and so the author of the Or Zarua — if the wheat kernel softened. And what the Rama wrote here that we say here it was found, here it was — the Levush questioned: if the wheat kernel fell there automatically it was not in water and is not forbidden at all — difficulty on the Rama; the Rama's intent here is that we are concerned lest a wheat kernel fell like a chametz kernel from elsewhere into this matzah — here it was found and here it was. And the Levush explained there is prohibition only from the wheat kernel to baked matzah, but in dough he permits because it does not leaven, and wrote the custom is not like the Rama's words to forbid in dough or in matzah that is not baked. And behold in the Tur he compared here dough to baked matzah, and it implies that if it had been in water there would be prohibition; and certainly the author of the Levush did not see the books' words that we mentioned, that forbid even in dough; and the essence is as Magen Avraham wrote here:",
    "12:א": "(א) If they scalded a chicken, etc. Stam scalding is in a second vessel; and so explicitly in Sefer Rokeach siman 211:",
    "12:ב": "(ב) The chicken is forbidden. For perhaps the wheat kernel was in water before scalding and leavened and absorbed from the water — so too in the Tur in the name of the Ramban; and it is difficult why we do not say here it was found here it was; and after scalding the chicken it came there. And it appears he derives prohibition from impurity, for we say in chapter Kol Me'arvin (Eruvin page 35): one touched one at night and does not know if alive or dead, and the next day he found it dead — the Sages render impure, for all impurities are as at the time of finding; and for this reason Rokeach wrote: a wheat kernel found in baked matzah and it is unknown if it leavened while still dough or never leavened — that matzah alone is forbidden, but other matzot we establish on their presumption and permit; here too we say the water retroactively as at the time of finding now; and one should not say here too the chicken on its presumption, for since the water was certainly in those and certainly leavens, and the chicken was certainly there — its presumption was impaired, unlike other matzot above, for even though we say retroactively as now, that is only in that matzah, but it is not necessary for other matzot, as appears to me. And one should not say it is double uncertainty that perhaps the wheat kernel was digested in the chicken — as the Rama wrote afterward one can say it deals with a whole wheat kernel; and also one should not say perhaps it did not leaven, since one who is expert can discern — this is not called a sufficient uncertainty, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 84:",
    "12:ג": "(ג) An embedded wheat kernel. It implies but if it is not embedded there is no prohibition, for it is double uncertainty — perhaps it did not leaven, and if you say it leavened, perhaps afterward it fell; all the more so here where it is visible that it is not embedded — only we are stringent from uncertainty; but the Rama wrote nearby seif 9 that we forbid in any amount even if they did not split open; and it appears here where there is double uncertainty we permit selling the chicken to a gentile, and we are stringent only after the second uncertainty, like that seif 9:",
    "12:ד": "(ד) If it is during Pesach. This is written according to his answer to the Tur's difficulty contradicting itself in seif 11; but according to what is difficult anyway, it is permitted here even on Pesach, for cold does not impart taste at all:",
    "12:ה": "(ה) By force of double uncertainty. The author of the Or Zarua questioned that this is not double uncertainty, for the mind of fools is not a mind, and it is possible that one who is expert knows to assess that it was not digested; and I do not know the difficulty, for this matter cannot be known — it is like entities in a full stomach; and all such cases are certainly uncertainty, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 81. And one can say: here in this seif regarding grain on top he wrote the Rama that one may not permit for eating because of double uncertainty; and one should not say here there is another leniency that there is only rabbinic prohibition in any amount, but regarding grain there is uncertainty in all the grain — this is not so, for above too in some places it implies the majority is permitted; and so is the law in that very responsum of the Rashba that the Beit Yosef brings in a year when rains were abundant — what is written in Shulchan Aruch seif 4, and there it is explained that the majority is permitted; and further, wherever double uncertainty helps, it helps even for d'oraisa prohibition; and therefore it appears he was stringent there for eating only because there is possibility without loss — it is like a matter that has permitters; and as the author concluded in that law that we copied in seif 2, concluding: since there is no loss in keeping it, etc. — unlike food needed for Pesach, where we follow practical halachah, as appears to me. Rokeach wrote: a piece of chametz was found in a bucket of water and it was unknown when they took the water from the bucket whether chametz was in it or not, and they cooked in it and they poured out the cooked dish — but the water in the bucket is forbidden, for perhaps the gentile who brings water put chametz in at that hour — end of his words; and the Beit Yosef brought these words and they have no explanation; and it appears there is an error, and it should read: when they took water from the well — the intent is they drew water first from the well to cook, and afterward drew water in a bucket and chametz was found in the bucket, and it was unknown whether chametz was in the well when they drew to cook — automatically they cooked in chametz water; with this he ruled the cooked dish permitted, but the water in the bucket alone is forbidden, for the gentile drawing to the bucket put chametz at that hour, but that chametz was not in the well at all. And it is astonishing on the Beit Yosef who copied and did not notice; and how can it be — difficult, for it implies if the chametz was before it was cooked it would be forbidden, but chametz in cold does not forbid, as above; and it is possible he holds like the Rashba that the Beit Yosef brings, who argues on the Rosh and holds cold also forbids, and we hold like the Rosh and Tur that cold does not forbid; and it appears if chametz soaked in cold water from the eve — forbidden to all, for soaked is like cooked; and so the author of the Or Zarua in the name of Rashal. The Tur wrote, and this is its language in Sefer Rokeach: a wheat kernel found in a pot — the spoon with which they stirred in it is forbidden; if he placed it in another pot, all are forbidden; and the Beit Yosef on this: it is simple, and why did he not copy it in Shulchan Aruch? And I say this requires deep study, for at first glance this passage is not halachah, for the Talmud Yerushalmi siman 93 in the name of Rabbeinu Tam wrote: what is forbidden in any amount does not impart taste, and any amount from it does not forbid in any amount afterward — meaning from what it releases; and as the Tur wrote there: even if forbidden in its species in any amount — for R' Yehuda we do not say two any-amounts; and if so here, where the spoon forbidden from the pot containing a wheat kernel is forbidden because of any amount — why should it forbid another pot in any amount? And so in the Levush siman 92: chametz and yeast forbidden in any amount do not impart taste, and if it fell again into another pot it is nullified in the majority if unrecognized, and if recognized one discards and the rest is permitted — end of his words; and there we wrote that this if unrecognized nullification is not the intent, and they said this only when recognized one discards; and its release is not forbidden in any amount since it itself was forbidden in any amount; and if so this case here is very difficult; and it appears to me to answer properly: here with the spoon we are concerned lest the spoon touched the wheat kernel itself in the pot and was forbidden from it, not from the cooked dish in the pot. And we find such in Yoreh De'ah siman 95 regarding bowls washed in a dairy churn — they forbid there because the bowls touch the griddle and receive taste from it, and we do not follow the water in the griddle; here too the spoon receives from the wheat kernel itself; and one should not say here as well that the way of the spoon is to touch everything in the pot when stirring, and it was forbidden by taste imparted, not by any amount, for there had to be sixty in what was absorbed in the spoon against the wheat kernel; and it is possible there is not so much absorption, even though we assess the whole spoon everywhere — that is for stringency to assess against it because we do not know how much comes out, but for leniency no, for perhaps there is not so much absorption that there would be sixty in it against the wheat kernel — therefore it properly forbids (but if one inserts) another spoon into that pot that was forbidden because of inserting the first spoon — that spoon is not forbidden so as to forbid another pot when they insert it, since it is forbidden only in any amount from the cooked dish, it does not forbid others in any amount; and similarly if they removed the wheat kernel from the pot it fell in and someone inserted a spoon — it does not forbid afterward by insertion into another pot in any amount, unless there is sixty against the spoon, and it is permitted, as appears to me clearly; and this is a peg that will not move in seif 4:",
    "13:_": "Into the pit during Pesach. In the Tur it is written stam, but the Beit Yosef explained it deals during Pesach, for if before Pesach behold it was nullified before Pesach and does not return and revive; and if so Shulchan Aruch holds like the first view in siman 447 seif 4; and the author of the Or Zarua wrote that nevertheless l'chatchila it is forbidden even before Pesach, since it is possible to draw water from another pit; and in my humble opinion here sometimes it is forbidden even b'dieved even before Pesach, for the leavened water remained above — even though a little mixed above, nevertheless they stood and endured and are as if in their own substance, and it resembles that law in R' Yehuda siman 92 regarding did not stir and covered, for it does not mix entirely except if they already drew from there several times before — then it is like stirring the pot and it is nullified before Pesach; but during Pesach there is no distinction, as appears to me. And the Mordechai wrote in chapter 20: this law of hag'alah of a pot also applies; and afterward wrote: but wheat that fell into a pit of water, etc., that it does not leaven while the wheat kernel is cold — end of his words. Once a servant brought a vessel with honey called mead to drink and a wheat kernel was found in it; I said it does not forbid in cold, even though it leavened, as the Tur wrote: since the water is cold the wheat kernel does not impart taste in them, and even full chametz that fell into a pit is permitted for this reason — end of his words. And even though it is written in Shulchan Aruch seif 12 that some forbid during Pesach — we already proved there is no distinction found except the Beit Yosef wrote thus as an answer to explain the Rosh's words, and we are not concerned lest it was in water, etc.; and we already mentioned the difficulty; and if so we do not find anyone who disagrees on this matter; and on the contrary, from the Mordechai we mentioned nearby it is proven that on Pesach itself the wheat was in the well, and nevertheless he wrote it does not impart taste in cold; and also there is no concern here lest the wheat kernel was when the honey was boiling, for then what was released from it was already nullified before Pesach and does not return and revive — even though the wheat kernel is still inside, nevertheless at that time it was cold and does not impart taste, as stated:",
    "14:א": "(א) One peels all of them. In responsum of the Mishna Berurah siman 69 he disagrees on this, that peeling is not required for the rest, for if because of concern lest they touched the wheat kernel itself we do not establish prohibition except here it was found here it was; and he expanded somewhat on this. And it is astonishing on him that he did not examine Shulchan Aruch in Yoreh De'ah siman 105 seif 9 that wrote there: and if it is unknown whether milk touched all of them, all of them, etc. Further difficulty: here peeling is not required at all, since the wheat kernel does not spread at all like a weak thing; and he expanded that we are not stringent on chametz on Pesach to say it spreads. And certainly this is true, as we mentioned in siman 461 seif 4 regarding adhesion to matzah — only that case is different, for there is nothing that spreads entirely, unlike when there is meat that spreads — we are concerned lest it went and seasoned the wheat kernel and it returns to spread in the meat, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 98 seif 5; and even a thin piece of prohibition and the permitted of its kind, etc. — we can say here too with matzah and meat; but I already cast a stone on this passage in Yoreh De'ah there. And I mentioned Rashal's words that in salting at least we do not say this reasoning; and nevertheless peeling is required even for the rest because of concern for contact, as stated; and in that responsum he mentioned there that wherever peeling is required and not possible it is permitted like Ra'avad's view. And I brought bundled proofs in Yoreh De'ah siman 91 that we do not hold like Ra'avad, but sixty is required against the peel:",
    "14:ב": "(ב) And there are those who are stringent. The reason we hold in all prohibitions salting is like cooking — so too regarding chametz; and the Rama decided to be lenient in the other pieces, since also in other prohibitions it is only a stringency of the custom that peeling suffices in salting according to most poskim; and therefore on chametz on Pesach, which is rabbinic prohibition in any amount, one may be lenient; and it would have been fit to be lenient even in that piece that peeling should suffice; only since we see the wheat kernel and it rests on it, one should be stringent on it like the stringent ones; and there is an error in what he wrote, etc. — and it should read to sell everything to a gentile, even though we do not practice thus in other chametz mixtures, as in siman 447; and he means that here in salting it is more lenient than other chametz mixtures mentioned in siman 447. And for the law we saw dispute among the early ones in the Mordechai chapter 20 whether to forbid in salting like cooking; and the later ones — Rashal in responsum siman 79 — rule to burn everything, and so too the author of She'erit Yosef and the author of the Or Zarua. And the Mishna Berurah disagrees with him to rule like the lenient ones, and he is lenient in other pieces even without peeling; and the Rama in responsum siman 128 wrote to permit other pieces, and this is its language: even one who wishes to be stringent should rule as he heard some sages instruct to burn that piece and keep the others after Pesach; and in my humble opinion one should practice thus: if a wheat kernel is found on salted dry meat without brine, one may permit keeping everything this way — where it was found one peels, and the rest one keeps, since there is only uncertainty of contact and one does not eat from it on Pesach but keeps it for after Pesach; and this certainly has no stumbling block, since for many great early and later authorities it is eaten, and we do not keep it; but if the wheat kernel is found inside the brine of meat or fish, then it is considered like cooked, and all the more so if it rested there from the eve — its law is as all that rests in the brine is as if cooked in one pot and all is forbidden and requires destruction, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 105 regarding soaked in brine or vinegar that in a small measure it is like cooked; but what rests above the brine one may also keep until after Pesach, for spreading does not go upward in brine, as explained in Yoreh De'ah seif 9 that there is no spreading upward in brine; and therefore what touches the meat in the brine one peels, and the rest one keeps or does as the Rama wrote at the end to sell everything to a gentile; and it appears here too one should peel the place of the wheat kernel first, for this is full chametz, as appears to me; and this is when it is not in the brine, but if it is in brine it is forbidden to sell, for it is full chametz, and one should do as written nearby. And what Shulchan Aruch wrote in this seif: if an embedded wheat kernel is found — it is plain that for us the law is thus if it softened, even though it is not embedded, as stated:",
    "16:_": "The bird is permitted. For roasting does not forbid more than peeling, and the crop is peeling — so too in the Beit Yosef in the name of the author of the Or Zarua; and even though we hold roasting requires sixty, here where spreading in a wheat kernel is not applicable peeling suffices; and further, searing is better than roasting — therefore even for us this bird is permitted:",
    "2:א": "(א) For since they rested at ease. The Tur wrote this in the name of R' Eliezer: even though regarding what descends below they are not forbidden unless they split open — that case is different, for there the Torah law is under discussion, therefore they do not come to leavening until they split open; but here, since they rested at ease, they come to leavening; and the Ran in the name of Ra'ah wrote that here splitting open is not required, for it deals with wheat that splits open in its natural growth, and specifically regarding barley splitting open is required; and Ra'am distinguished between small dripping and abundant soaking, as explained in the Beit Yosef; and since he mentioned here the view of R' Efrayim, it implies that we hold there is no distinction between wheat and barley nor between small dripping and abundant soaking.",
    "2:ב": "(ב) By force of double uncertainty. It requires further study regarding the Rama's view in this, for he wrote these words — the Agur brought them in the Beit Yosef regarding wheat over which water passed via the roof, that we rely on the permission that Ra'avyah wrote; and the Mordechai brought at length in the name of Ra'avyah to eat some of them before Pesach or to sell to a gentile little by little and the rest is permitted by force of double uncertainty — perhaps water did not descend upon them, and if you say water descended upon them, perhaps they did not leaven (meaning: were not leavened); but the lower wheat that is near the floor of the attic — it is nearly certain that water descended there to the thickness of a finger or half a finger, and those one discards and the rest is permitted to keep; but not to eat from them on Pesach, for even though regarding it shall not be seen uncertainties are nullified in their majority regarding eating, which is in any amount — we are not lenient — end of his words. That which he wrote to eat some of them, etc. — it appears there is an error in his words, for this permission to eat some of them the author of the Or Zarua brought in the name of Ra'avyah in the lengthy Mordechai — that it is permitted even on Pesach, for we can say it is a double uncertainty: perhaps they already ate from the forbidden ones, and if you say they did not eat, one can say they will now eat from the permitted — as with a ring of idolatry that became mixed and one fell into the Great Sea and all were permitted to benefit together, for certainly there is permission; and even if they did not eat from it, one may take from it and throw it into the sea, or eat from them before Pesach, and the rest are permitted; and there is no nullifying prohibition l'chatchila since it is before Pesach, and you have no matzah that is not more than the prohibition — two prohibitions — and in double uncertainty we follow leniency even for d'oraisa prohibition — end of his words. And in the Agur that we mentioned he mixed two reasons for permission — namely to eat some of them, which applies even if there is certainly chametz among them; and he also mentioned double uncertainty from perhaps they did not leaven — and it appears to me there is some leap in his words. And in any case, since the essence of the permission here is by force of double uncertainty, why is it forbidden in eating on Pesach? For even in d'oraisa prohibition it is permitted in double uncertainty — all the more so in rabbinic prohibition in any amount; and further, the Rama himself who wrote here that double uncertainty does not help for eating — he wrote in siman 453 seif 3: if there is grain among them that sprouted — for if there are sixty in what did not sprout it is permitted if one grinds them, and that is even to eat on Pesach — behold they do not say it returns and revives; and why did he rule here forbidden because it returns and revives? And necessarily one must say that here it deals where there are not sixty against the prohibition, and the essence of his emphasis teaches that it is not permitted even in double uncertainty. First difficulty: why does double uncertainty not help here in truth? Further, Ra'avyah truly permitted because of double uncertainty even for eating, as stated in his name, and also in the next seif it is proven thus — it requires study. And for the law, it implies even according to all that the prohibition for eating is because there is no loss in keeping them — it implies that if there is loss, such as when he has no other grain, it is permitted even in eating.",
    "4:_": "We are not concerned for stam, etc. — so wrote the Rashba in a responsum that the Beit Yosef brought, and this is its language: and even those stacks that we saw rain fell upon them — we suspend to leniency that perhaps water did not enter except in the upper sheaves but not in the belly of the stack; and if you say it entered, perhaps it did not have time to leaven as long as it did not come in water — therefore there is double uncertainty, and for leniency — end of his words. From this too there is contradiction to the Rama's ruling nearby, for behold here it is permitted even in eating:",
    "8:א": "(א) There are those who are stringent. It is the Mordechai that we are concerned lest flour or fine flour was mixed in. And the Beit Yosef wrote that there is no prohibition in this, for it is fruit juice and only fine flour leavens; and it is possible that they are concerned that it is full chametz since they knead wheat first. And the Rosh wrote in a responsum general 24 that he did not see a custom of prohibition in honey, for mixing flour is not common and also it was nullified before Pesach; and if we come to be concerned because of mixtures we should forbid it all year — some say they put nevelah meat into it and it turns into honey and is permitted, for after the fact we follow — end of his words. And nevertheless the Rama concludes that one may not eat it on Pesach except what they bring from the hive, even though in the Mordechai he forbids this too because of substitution with other honey — it is an extra stringency. And I saw in many places that they eat it, and also in the holy community of Krakow there is no custom to forbid — unlike the Rama's words here that one should not eat honey except what they bring from the hive that they make a drink from, etc. — meaning even to eat it as is it is permitted, for they make a drink from it l'chatchila; but one should not say that specifically the drink is permitted because it was made before Pesach, for he did not mention before Pesach; and further, he began with eating — therefore this too even in eating is permitted, only that he mentioned the drink as evidence that people practice leniency. And based on this it appears to me there is full permission to cook on chol ha-mo'ed a drink called mead from honey that is recognizable as coming from the hive to the sight of people — anything fit for drinking on Yom Tov; even though the author of the Or Zarua wrote to forbid making the drink on the festival because of concern for mixtures, and wrote that so they practiced — nevertheless it appears to me that one who is lenient for the honor of Yom Tov in these countries where we have no other drink on Pesach has not lost; and there is no applicable custom of prohibition here, for each one prepares for himself before Pesach; but if one comes to cook on the festival and has no drink other than that — one should not protest him, and honoring Yom Tov is in his hand, as appears to me. And based on the words of the Rosh's responsum that we mentioned in the name of R' Yonah, it appears to teach merit on what is written in Shulchan Aruch Yoreh De'ah siman 114 that the Rashba was careful from saffron because they mix dry meat in it; and according to R' Yonah's words there is permission since the saffron dissolves in food and the prohibition is nullified, and it is literally like honey here all year:",
    "8:ב": "(ב) And dried figs. For there is concern they scatter flour on them; and even those that grew in his garden are forbidden because of what an observer sees — because of other figs; and the prohibition of grapes is that they are placed near figs:",
    "8:ג": "(ג) And tsuker kandil. This is not what we call tsuker kandil — that has more concern and is full chametz, for they cook white tsuker in sour vats and it reddens; and even to keep it is forbidden because there is a mixture of chametz. Rather it is tsuker kandiya that Maharil wrote they ate on the last Yom Tov, and R' Nasan gave the students tsuker he brought from Candia that was clear to them there is no chametz in it. And it is difficult why they did not decree because of other tsuker like with figs; and it appears that tsuker is a distinct species and its form is recognizable, and we do not decree on it; and it appears this is what we call kaner or hot tsuker that certainly has no chametz and no cooking was done on it — only they bring it as its natural product; that is what they eat on the last Yom Tov, as stated:"
  }
};
